# Crytek Sponza test fixture — license + provenance

## Project's adopted license

This fixture is distributed under the **Creative Commons Attribution 3.0
Unported (CC BY 3.0) License** — https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/3.0/

Attribution chain:
  - Original Sponza model (Dabrovic Sponza):
      Marko Dabrovic, 2002.
  - Crytek Sponza remodel (this fixture — the "improved" 2010 version):
      Frank Meinl, Crytek, August 19, 2010.
      http://www.crytek.com/cryengine/cryengine3/downloads (historical)
  - Archive fixes + hosting (bump maps re-computed from normal maps,
    mask textures merged into diffuse alpha, missing gi_flag.tga created,
    stray untextured banner removed):
      Morgan McGuire, July 14, 2011.
      https://casual-effects.com/g3d/data10/common/model/crytek_sponza/sponza.zip
  - Vendored into objtomc on 2026-07-04 (Session 36; this commit).

## Upstream license claims (verbatim)

The upstream `sponza.zip` at the McGuire Computer Graphics Archive
contains TWO claims that must be reconciled. Both are reproduced
verbatim below.

### Claim 1: archive-level catalog + wider-community attribution

The McGuire Computer Graphics Archive catalog + the wider graphics
community (e.g. `github.com/yuna0x0/sbox-classic-sponza/LICENSE.md`)
declare the Crytek Sponza fixture:

    License: CC BY 3.0
    © 2010 Frank Meinl, Crytek

### Claim 2: `copyright.txt` inside the zip

The following is the verbatim content of `copyright.txt` from inside
the upstream zip. It is the only license-related text file the
zip contains:

    July 14, 2011 Morgan McGuire modified the model from Crytek's OBJ
    export to correct some small errors.  He computed bump maps from the
    normal maps using <a
    href="http://cs.williams.edu/~morgan/code/">normal2bump.cpp</a> (since
    MTL files expect height bumps, not normals), put the "mask" textures
    into the alpha channel of the associated diffuse texture, cleaned up
    noise in the masks, created the missing gi_flag.tga texture, and
    removed the long untextured banner floating in the middle of the
    atrium that appears in the file but in none of the published images of
    the model.  The banner is in banner.obj.



    http://www.crytek.com/cryengine/cryengine3/downloads


    Sponza Model
    August 19, 2010
    The Atrium Sponza Palace, Dubrovnik, is an elegant and improved model created by Frank Meinl. The original Sponza model was created by Marko Dabrovic in early 2002. Over the years, the Sponza Atrium scene has become one of the most popular 3D scenes for testing global illumination and radiosity due to it's specific architectural structure which is particularly complex for global illumination light.

    However, nowadays it is considered as a simple model, thus it was decided to crate a new model with highly improved appearance and scene complexity. It is donated to the public for  radiosity and is represented in several different formats (3ds, Obj) for use with various commercial 3D applications and renderers.


    Screenshot from the I3D paper
    http://crytek.com/sites/default/files/20100301_lpv.pdf

## Resolution

The archive catalog + wider-community references declare CC BY 3.0;
the zip's `copyright.txt` says "donated to the public for radiosity"
(permissive, but not explicitly CC BY 3.0). This is the same pattern
as the Cornell Box fixture (`tests/fixtures/cornell_box/LICENSE`),
and we resolve it identically — conservatively in favor of the stricter
CC BY 3.0 reading.

Rationale (per `research/scale_tier4_proposal.md` §1.5.3 +
`DECISIONS.md` 2026-07-04 "Scale Tier-4 scope (v0.2 work item #2)"):

  1. CC BY 3.0 is the stricter of the two readings. "Donated to the
     public" doesn't forbid attribution; CC BY 3.0 requires it. The
     strict reading subsumes the permissive reading.
  2. Attribution is good practice regardless. The Crytek Sponza has
     been the de-facto architectural test scene in computer graphics
     research for 15+ years; the wider community treats it as CC BY
     3.0. Ignoring attribution to claim public-domain status would be
     legally aggressive and socially uncollegial toward Frank Meinl,
     Crytek, Marko Dabrovic, and Morgan McGuire.
  3. Matches the project's established vendoring pattern (Cornell Box
     + Stanford bunny precedents): each vendored upstream gets a
     LICENSE record with verbatim upstream-license text +
     attribution + re-derivation provenance.

Anyone redistributing these files (in source or compiled form) must
preserve the CC BY 3.0 attribution. The full CC BY 3.0 license text
is at https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/3.0/legalcode.

